# Grade six's coding puzzles, one on each thing the year added: spaces in text, a cooling model, a tank
# that stops a minute late, an otherwise at the wrong depth, a swap with no spare name, a spiral that
# stops early, an orbit one step out, a repeat that goes round for nothing, a sort with a pass too few,
# and a long program folded into three blocks. The moon's base is where the puzzles are set.
lesson coding-g6-coding-puzzles v=1 format=puzzles grade=6 unit=9 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Coding puzzles"
  goal "Find what a program really does, name the one line to change, and make a program shorter where it can be."

  puzzle stars=1 {
    show coding.puzzle-spaces n=4 k=0 a=2 easy-n=3 hard-k=1 hard-a=5
  }
  puzzle stars=1 {
    show coding.puzzle-cool n=2 g=64 easy-g=32 hard-n=3 hard-g=32
  }
  puzzle stars=1 {
    show coding.puzzle-tank c=80 r=10 easy-c=60 hard-c=90 hard-r=15
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show coding.puzzle-inner k=1 easy-k=0
    say "An otherwise belongs to the if it lines up with."
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show coding.puzzle-swap k=1 easy-k=0
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show coding.puzzle-self s=6 c=2 easy-s=5 hard-s=7 hard-c=3
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show coding.puzzle-orbit k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show coding.puzzle-steps n=10 a=5 m=30 easy-a=4 easy-m=20 hard-n=15
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show coding.puzzle-passes k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show coding.puzzle-fewest a=2 easy-a=1 hard-a=3
  }

  grown-ups """
    Each puzzle is a program from this year that runs but does not do what was meant, or does it in
    more steps than it needs. The task is to say what it really does and then name the one line or
    number to change. The wrong idea to watch for is guessing a fix before running the program by
    hand; in the child's working, look for the numbers written down as the program would make them.
    If the child is stuck, ask what the program was meant to do in words, then run it one line at a
    time and stop at the first line whose result is a surprise; stay on a puzzle another day rather
    than guessing. Off the page, give a recipe that says to stir once when it meant stir every
    minute, and ask which step to change. The last two puzzles have no single method: which list a
    sort with a pass too few gets wrong, and a long program folded into three blocks.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the fish repeat goes round three times, the cooling runs for twenty minutes, the tank holds 60 litres at 10 a minute, the otherwise puzzle is on a cold day, the swap uses 5 and 2, the spiral stops at 2, the orbit turns 40 degrees a step, the repeat for nothing adds 4 to reach 20, the sort's lists have the shortest plank last in one of them, and the square to fold has sides of 1."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the fish line should run inside the repeat after a say, the cooling runs for thirty minutes and ends colder than the air, the tank fills 15 a minute, the otherwise puzzle is on a day above freezing, the spiral stops at 3, the orbit turns 24 degrees a step, the repeat for nothing goes round 15 times, the sort's lists include one in reverse order, and the square to fold has sides of 3."
  }
}
